    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "25-28",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays keep finding ways to make a promising stretch feel unfinished - then Sunday's punch to the gut arrived and reminded everyone how thin the margin is. <strong>Toronto fell 4-1 to the Pirates on May 24, ending a three-game surge and slipping to 25-28, third in the AL East, 10.5 games back.</strong> That is the ledger now: a club with enough life to sweep the Yankees on May 21 and take the first two from Pittsburgh, but not enough consistency to carry the momentum through the weekend. Vladimir Guerrero Jr. supplied the most concerning note when he exited after being hit by a pitch, and that kind of bruise matters for a lineup that cannot afford to lose its middle-order muscle. The Blue Jays are still clinging to a playoff seed, but the record tells the sharper truth - this is a team chasing the pack, not setting the pace. The next chance to steady the book comes fast, with Miami due in Toronto on Monday night. <strong>What matters now is whether the Jays answer Sunday's flat bat with a clean, hard-edged start against the Marlins.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 25, 2026"
      },
