      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The draft board has finally stopped spinning, and Washington's real season now begins in the quieter, uglier business of building a roster that can hold up in September. <strong>The Commanders left the draft with a class meant to harden the middle of the team and add cheap, controllable depth</strong> - exactly what a club sitting 3rd in the NFC East, 6 games back and parked at playoff seed 14 needs more than noise. There are no games to chase and no standings to game; this is about fit, not flash. The rookie class has to help on the line, in the trenches, and wherever Dan Quinn's staff sees a hole that can be patched before camp opens. That is the story now: who can play early, who can survive special teams, and which undrafted signings can turn a Friday night transaction into a real Sunday role. Washington's best path is plain enough - find one or two rookies who can force their way into the depth chart and keep the roster from thinning out under injury and attrition. <strong>What to watch next is whether this draft class starts looking like a bridge to better football - or just another stack of names on paper</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 25, 2026"
